## 1. Decision framework: where heat reuse should go first

HeatOS scores both candidate sites on nine weighted criteria (1-5 scale).

| Criterion | Weight | Chelsea | Lansing | Why |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---|
{% for c in scores.criteria -%}
| {{ c.label }} | {{ (c.weight * 100) | round | int }}% | {{ c.chelsea }} | {{ c.lansing }} | {{ c.rationale }} |
{% endfor -%}
| **Weighted total** | 100% | **{{ "%.2f" | format(scores.totals.chelsea) }}** | **{{ "%.2f" | format(scores.totals.lansing) }}** | |

**Robustness.** Across {{ "{:,}".format(scores.robustness.n_draws) }} random re-weightings (Dirichlet, alpha = weight x {{ scores.robustness.alpha_scale | int }}) with +/-{{ scores.robustness.score_noise }} noise on every score, **Chelsea ranks first in {{ "%.1f" | format(scores.robustness.p_chelsea_wins * 100) }}% of draws**. Recommendation: **{{ scores.recommended | title }}**.

Within the chosen site, a building connects only if its best option has a positive 20-year value (financial NPV at {{ "%.0f" | format(cfg.finance.discount_rate.value * 100) }}% plus avoided CO2 at ${{ carbon_price | int }}/t), and only while the data center has firm capacity left. Buildings are added in priority order: NPV x (1 + equity score) x boiler urgency.

